use cases of the case statements that I usually use in my projects. So let's start.

3
00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:18.219
The main purpose of the case statement is to do data transformations, and data transformations

4
00:00:18.219 --> 00:00:21.399
is a very important process in each data project.

5
00:00:21.399 --> 00:00:25.860
And one very important task in data transformations are that we can generate new information.

6
00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:30.899
We can go and create new columns based on the existing data that we have in the database

7
00:00:30.899 --> 00:00:32.700
using the case statements.

8
00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:37.779
And this of course can help us deriving new information for our analysis without modifying

9
00:00:37.779 --> 00:00:40.819
the source database only for analytics.

10
00:00:40.819 --> 00:00:45.560
So my friends, the main purpose of the case statement is to do data transformations by

11
00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:48.279
creating and generating new columns.

12
00:00:48.279 --> 00:00:52.619
So now let's start with the first use case, and the most important and famous one is,

13
00:00:52.619 --> 00:00:56.020
we use case statements in order to categorize the data.

14
00:00:56.020 --> 00:01:01.619
This means we are going to group up the data into different categories based on certain conditions.

15
00:01:01.619 --> 00:01:03.939
And now you might ask why this use case is important.

16
00:01:03.939 --> 00:01:09.900
Well, classifying and grouping data is fundamental in data analysis and reporting because it

17
00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:13.500
makes the data easier to understand and as well to track.

18
00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:18.580
But what's more important, it's gonna help us aggregating the data based on the categories.

19
00:01:18.580 --> 00:01:22.980
Alright, so now let's have the following task and it says, generate a report showing

20
00:01:22.980 --> 00:01:26.059
total sales for each of the following categories.

21
00:01:26.059 --> 00:01:32.300
Category high if the sales is over 50, category medium if the sales is between 20 and 50,

22
00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:34.819
and low if the sales is 20 or less.

23
00:01:34.819 --> 00:01:37.940
And sort the categories from the highest sales to the lowest.

24
00:01:37.940 --> 00:01:42.459
Ok, so let's do it step by step, and now before we do any data aggregations, we have to go

25
00:01:42.459 --> 00:01:46.660
and create a new column called categories because we don't have it in the database.

26
00:01:46.739 --> 00:01:49.260
So now let's start with a very simple select statement.

27
00:01:49.260 --> 00:01:50.900
So select, what do we need?

28
00:01:50.900 --> 00:01:54.860
Let's take the order ID, the sales, and that's it for now.

29
00:01:54.860 --> 00:01:59.019
So from sales, orders, let's go and execute it.

30
00:01:59.019 --> 00:02:04.980
And now we have our 10 orders and we have to go and now create a new column called categories.

31
00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:07.019
And we're gonna do that using the case statements.

32
00:02:07.019 --> 00:02:12.380
So let's take a new line and we start with case and then again a new line in order to

33
00:02:12.380 --> 00:02:14.940
define the first condition using the when.

34
00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:18.559
So the first condition is the high where sales is over 50.

35
00:02:18.559 --> 00:02:19.740
So it's very simple.

36
00:02:19.740 --> 00:02:25.660
So when the sales is higher than 50, what can happen if this is true?

37
00:02:25.660 --> 00:02:28.100
We want to show the value high.

38
00:02:28.100 --> 00:02:29.899
So this is the first condition.

39
00:02:29.899 --> 00:02:32.259
And then let's move to the second one.

40
00:02:32.259 --> 00:02:39.419
If the sales is higher than 20, that means it's less than 50 and higher than 20.

41
00:02:39.419 --> 00:02:42.199
Then we want to see the value medium.

42
00:02:42.199 --> 00:02:45.399
And now for the last category, the low, we don't have to go and create a condition for

43
00:02:45.399 --> 00:02:51.259
that because if those two fails, then that means the sales either equal to 20 or less.

44
00:02:51.259 --> 00:02:52.259
So what are we gonna do?

45
00:02:52.259 --> 00:02:57.000
We're gonna just do a simple else and show the value low like this.

46
00:02:57.000 --> 00:02:59.119
Let me make this a little bit smaller.

47
00:02:59.119 --> 00:03:02.679
Now what is missing in our case is of course the end.

48
00:03:02.679 --> 00:03:03.880
Without it, you're gonna get an error.

49
00:03:03.880 --> 00:03:07.160
So end and let's give it a name category.

50
00:03:07.160 --> 00:03:08.279
So we are ready.

51
00:03:08.279 --> 00:03:09.559
Let's go and execute it.

52
00:03:09.559 --> 00:03:11.259
So now let's check randomly stuff.

53
00:03:11.320 --> 00:03:13.740
So as you can see here, we have the sales of 50.

54
00:03:13.740 --> 00:03:15.539
It is low, which is correct.

55
00:03:15.539 --> 00:03:18.979
And then we have here 60, it's above 50 and we have the category high.

56
00:03:18.979 --> 00:03:23.220
And now if you check the order number six, we have the order 50, it's medium because

57
00:03:23.220 --> 00:03:25.339
it is not higher than 50.

58
00:03:25.339 --> 00:03:27.419
It is between 50 and 20.

59
00:03:27.419 --> 00:03:31.779
So now as you can see, we have now classified our orders using the category.

60
00:03:31.779 --> 00:03:34.740
Now the next step is that we're gonna go and aggregate the data.

61
00:03:34.740 --> 00:03:35.940
So how are we gonna do that?

62
00:03:35.940 --> 00:03:38.119
We will use a sub query.

63
00:03:38.119 --> 00:03:39.119
So let's do it like this.

64
00:03:39.119 --> 00:03:42.639
So we're gonna go and select, and of course we're gonna group up the data by the category.

65
00:03:42.639 --> 00:03:47.000
So we're gonna go and select that category and we need the total sales.

66
00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:53.399
That means we're gonna go and use the function sum for the sales and we're gonna call it total sales.

67
00:03:53.399 --> 00:03:55.899
So now we have to nest the queries together.

68
00:03:55.899 --> 00:04:04.600
So from, this is our query like this, and then we have to close it and group by.

69
00:04:04.600 --> 00:04:06.600
So we are grouping by the category.

70
00:04:06.839 --> 00:04:11.199
Okay, so with that, we are now aggregating the sales by that category.

71
00:04:11.199 --> 00:04:12.360
It's very simple.

72
00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:13.800
Let's go and execute it.

73
00:04:13.800 --> 00:04:15.800
So now in the result, we have only three categories.

74
00:04:15.800 --> 00:04:19.480
We don't have the 10 orders because now we are doing data aggregations.

75
00:04:19.480 --> 00:04:22.760
So now the granularity now on the level of category.

76
00:04:22.760 --> 00:04:29.160
So now we can see the total sales for the high is 2010, the low we have 65 and the medium we have 105.

77
00:04:29.160 --> 00:04:33.679
And of course we are not done yet because in the task it says, sort the categories from

78
00:04:33.679 --> 00:04:35.839
the highest sales to the lowest.

79
00:04:36.079 --> 00:04:39.920
So that means we have to go and use an order by statement at the end, and we're going to

80
00:04:39.920 --> 00:04:45.279
sort the data by the sales from the highest to the lowest, that means descending. So that's it.

81
00:04:45.279 --> 00:04:46.559
Let's go and execute it.

82
00:04:46.559 --> 00:04:48.640
And now with that, we have our reports.

83
00:04:48.640 --> 00:04:52.119
Now we are showing the total sales by the categories and the data is sorted from the

84
00:04:52.119 --> 00:04:53.660
